 @lycherasbry  Clock speed between the 2 isn't what's causing AMD to draw more power, I'll explain where that power efficiency is coming from. See how AMD has the X3D CPUs and they're very power efficient for memory sensitive workloads like gaming? because with the large L3 cache more data is able to be kept closer to the CPU so it doesn't have to work as hard getting data from RAM as often, well what most people fail to see is that starting from the RTX 40 series Nvidia massively increased the L2 cache on their GPUs, typically you'd normally just see low single digit L2 cache on these GPUs, but now they're in the double digits.

Basically the 5070 TI has 48MB of L2 while the 9070 XT only has 8MB, now with that being said the 9070 XT does have a massive 64MB of L3 while the 5070 TI doesn't have any L3 cache but L3 is still further away than L2 and thus it's technically still slower to access L3 compared to L2 so it has to work harder compared to if it had a bigger L2 cache.

Had Nvidia kept using small L2 cache memory then the 5070 TI would pretty much always be hitting it's 300W power limit.

